Microscopic Colitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Chronic watery non-bloody diarrhoea, Macroscopically normal colonoscopy, Intraepithelial lymphocytosis/collagen band on biopsy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-044
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting, profuse watery diarrhoea occurring up to 10 times a day and night, causing severe sleep deprivation, profound exhaustion, and mild dehydration.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted chronic, high-frequency watery diarrhoea severely disrupting sleep and causing signs of systemic exhaustion and mild volume depletion in an older adult female. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to assess intravascular volume status, rule out an acute infectious exacerbation, and initiate aggressive symptomatic control to prevent progression to hypovolaemic collapse prior to standard clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am passing pure water all day and all night, I haven't slept properly in weeks, and I feel completely drained of all energy."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 2-month history of chronic, unremitting, profuse watery non-bloody diarrhoea. A key feature is nocturnal bowel movements resulting in severe sleep disruption and exhaustion. Reports intense urgency and occasional faecal incontinence. Describes diffuse, mild crampy abdominal pain and discomfort, but absolutely no severe, sharp, or localized pain. Notes a 3kg unintentional weight loss and profound generalized fatigue. Complete absence of rectal bleeding or macroscopic purulent exudate. Concurrent history of autoimmune thyroiditis (Hashimoto's). History of utilizing daily NSAIDs (Ibuprofen) for osteoarthritis over the past year.
Functional Status: Ambulating slowly, severely fatigued, afraid to leave the house due to faecal urgency.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of dehydration, syncopal falls, and severe impact on quality of life.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically fatigued, mildly pale, and anxious regarding bathroom proximity.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, flat, and non-distended. Diffuse, mild tenderness present on deep palpation without any localized guarding, rigidity, or rebound peritonism. Active, normal-pitched bowel sounds noted. Rectal examination deferred based on the clear history of non-bloody, high-volume watery diarrhoea. Orthostatic vital signs demonstrate a mild drop in blood pressure upon standing, indicating mild volume depletion. The clinical presentation explicitly aligns with chronic watery non-bloody diarrhoea in an older female taking NSAIDs; a macroscopically normal colonoscopy with specific microscopic histological findings is required for definitive diagnosis.
Environmental Observations: Multiple boxes of over-the-counter ibuprofen on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking daily Ibuprofen 400mg TDS for joint pain, a known strong precipitant of microscopic colitis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Microscopic Colitis - Status: Exacerbated (Severe symptomatic flare driven by NSAID use)
Differentials:

Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn's/UC) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of visible blood or mucus, the lack of severe localized pain, and the prominent, unremitting nocturnal watery nature of the diarrhoea, though colonoscopy with biopsy is ultimately required to differentiate.

Infectious Diarrhoea (e.g., Giardia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic 2-month timeline and the specific patient demographic (older female with autoimmune history on NSAIDs), though stool cultures must be sent to definitively exclude chronic infection.

Coeliac Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The primarily watery rather than steatorrhoeic (fatty, floating) nature of the stool, though the concurrent autoimmune thyroiditis increases the risk and warrants formal serological screening.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Living alone with fall risk due to mild dehydration)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a classic clinical picture of microscopic colitis: an older female with chronic, unremitting, nocturnal watery diarrhoea, concomitant autoimmune disease, and chronic NSAID use. The profound sleep deprivation and mild volume depletion mandated an urgent out-of-hours evaluation to definitively exclude a rigid surgical abdomen, assess haemodynamic stability, and immediately initiate structured oral rehydration and aggressive anti-motility therapy. Identifying the likely pharmacological trigger (NSAIDs) and ceasing it immediately, combined with potent symptomatic relief, was necessary to stabilize the patient at home and prevent emergency admission for intravenous hydration.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Loperamide 4mg immediately, followed by 2mg after each loose stool (maximum 16mg daily). Initiated strict oral rehydration therapy. Issued urgent pathology request for stool MCS, C. difficile toxin, faecal calprotectin, and coeliac serology.
Positive Behavioral Change: Completely and permanently stop taking all ibuprofen or other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs immediately. These specific medications are highly toxic to the microscopic lining of the bowel in susceptible individuals; physically removing this chemical trigger is the single most effective step to stop the massive fluid leak causing the unrelenting diarrhoea.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Must see regular GP within 48 hours to review blood and stool results and secure an urgent Gastroenterology referral for colonoscopy and potential initiation of oral budesonide.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient becomes completely unable to stand due to dizziness, stops passing urine entirely for 8 hours, or if the diarrhoea suddenly becomes bloody, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A camera test (colonoscopy) where tiny tissue samples are taken is the only way to definitively confirm this microscopic inflammation.
Clinical Course: Stopping the ibuprofen and taking the anti-diarrhoeal medication should slowly reduce the frequency of the bowel movements over the next week. Definitive treatment often requires a specific targeted steroid medication prescribed by a specialist.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Sit on the edge of the bed for a full 60 seconds before standing up to use the bathroom tonight. The fluid loss has lowered the blood pressure, making a fainting fall highly likely if standing up too quickly.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lining of the large bowel appears completely normal to the naked eye, but under a microscope, it is severely inflamed by immune cells, likely triggered directly by the daily ibuprofen. This microscopic swelling completely destroys the bowel's ability to absorb water, turning all stool into pure liquid that leaks out continuously, day and night. The prescribed medication will artificially slow the bowel muscle, buying time for the water to be absorbed, while stopping the ibuprofen removes the root cause of the damage.
Actionable Steps: Keep a 1-liter bottle of specifically mixed oral rehydration solution next to the bed and take three slow sips every time a nocturnal bathroom visit occurs. Drinking plain water will not replace the specific salts lost through the bowel and will actually worsen the dangerous dehydration and exhaustion.

